\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}





problems:
- monads introduce heavy cognitive friction and hide the state of the program until it is explicitly questioned. Totality is partial lie because a failure is disguised as an admisable value wraped around the monadic construction.
- unhandled exceptions bubble up the code stack, burying the location of the error and breaking totality silently.



Functions in mainstream type systems frequently lie about their execution guarantees. When a function signature claims to return a type $\tau$, but can silently terminate via panic or bottom-type ($\bot$) pollution, the caller's static guarantees are violated.
